{"id":"vouch-xlha.6","title":"check_registry is one 760-line function of closures","description":"git-vouch:1341-2100. check_registry holds the whole trust model in one function: about 25 nested closures (record_at, keys_at, adding, is_signed, owners, check_signer, may_grant, lowered, judge, judge_tier, standing, tier_ends, anchor_ends, judge_endorsement, endorsed_at, held_at...). They share and mutate local dicts (people, which loses claimants at 1623; signed, judged, stood), and held_at escapes in CheckResult with all of that captured.

What goes wrong if it stays: kernel reviewers cannot review one phase without holding all 760 lines in mind. No phase can be unit-tested on its own, so every test builds a whole registry, which is part of why the suite is slow. Phase order is implicit: the revocation pass at 1627 relies on owns() over the key-ownership state built at 1555-1625, and tier judging relies on revoked. A contributor reordering blocks gets no error.

Fix: no new abstractions needed. Turn the phases already marked by comments into module-level functions that pass their inputs explicitly: _load_records (1390-1425), _person_continuity (1443-1535), _key_owners (1555-1625), _revocations (1627-1700), _warn_unchained (1702-1733), tiers (check_signer through tier_ends), endorsements (1975-2005), and keep held_at as the one closure. An alternative is one small class whose memo dicts are attributes. Either way, each phase's inputs show up in its signature.","status":"open","priority":3,"issue_type":"task","owner":"mark@reviewcommit.com","created_at":"2026-10-23T19:23:26.147621639Z","created_by":"agent","updated_at":"2026-10-23T19:23:26.147621639Z","labels":["readability","review"],"dependencies":[{"issue_id":"vouch-xlha.6","depends_on_id":"vouch-xlha","type":"parent-child","created_at":"2026-10-23T19:23:26.172948609Z","created_by":"agent","metadata":"{}"}]}

